  "sourceFix": {
    "file": ".github/workflows/security-scan.yml",
    "problem": "All eight runtime Dockerfiles default SECURITY_PATCH_EPOCH to unset and use it in the OS patch RUN, but the cached daily scan did not supply the argument. A cached patch layer can skip package refresh.",
    "change": "Set SECURITY_PATCH_EPOCH to the GitHub run ID and run attempt so each scan/retry invalidates the patch RUN and subsequent layers while retaining earlier build caches.",
    "implemented": true,
    "freshWorkflowAcceptancePending": true,
    "applicationDeploymentExecuted": false,
    "packageUpgradeChange": "Five Ubuntu runtime Dockerfiles upgrade all installed packages with --with-new-pkgs and no recommended packages; gateway/frontend upgrade all installed Alpine packages. Repository/distro/base pins and nonroot users are preserved. Dashboard already upgrades all installed Alpine packages.",
    "ubuntuNoninteractive": true,
    "runtimeDockerfilesChanged": 7,
    "freshFullApplicationCiAndDevReleasePending": true,
    "applicationSourceOrDependencyLocksChanged": false
  },
  "fixPlan": [
    "Validate every configured runtime Dockerfile consumes the argument and the workflow supplies a GitHub-generated value.",
    "Apply compatible installed-package upgrades from the existing distro repositories; verify reported fixable medium/low packages in current source images and the independently pinned dev release digests. Do not assume all reported fixed versions are available until the scan confirms them.",
    "Run all seven service suites/builds and CI/CodeQL, then dev release gates, independent Ready/traffic/config/job absence checks, frontend/browser authorization smoke and additive rollback protection.",
    "Run the eight-component dev workflow and verify patch RUN execution, HIGH/CRITICAL gates and successful stable-category SARIF uploads against its exact source.",
    "Independently re-read current and historical Trivy categories; map current findings to actual component/package/version/fix status and deployed digests before claiming applicability or remediation.",
    "Retain legacy-category evidence; do not silently dismiss or delete historical analyses. Any separately approved analysis cleanup must require complete successful current coverage and preserve review evidence.",
    "Keep the existing owner actions and exact 198-revision deletion approval separate; source-image scans do not certify all live digests or physical removal of old images/revisions.",
    "For remaining upstream-unfixed findings, preserve exact package/version/advisory/fix status and review actual exploit prerequisites and runtime exposure; do not dismiss them as fixed or delete historical evidence."
  ],
